\chapter{Getting Started: Installation, Build, and First
Program}\label{ch2-getting-started-installation-build-and-first-program}

This chapter takes you from an empty directory to a compiled, running
MuDSL program. The C++ header and D module are individually
self-contained; installation means making the chosen implementation
visible to its compiler. Default builds need only the corresponding
standard library. Native Lua embedding is an optional build described in
Chapter 35.

We will cover the requirements, how to obtain and include the header,
and how to compile a program directly with \texttt{g++} or
\texttt{clang++}. We will then look at how to consume the library
through CMake's \texttt{INTERFACE} target, and how to build and run the
bundled examples.

Finally, we will walk through the first example program in detail and
review the compile errors you are most likely to meet on day one. By the
end you will have a working MuDSL program and the confidence to read the
rest of the manual.

\section{Requirements}\label{ch2-requirements}

For \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}, the language requirement is C++20.
MuDSL relies on language features that did not exist before C++20. It
uses concepts, both in real constraints and in
\texttt{requires}-expressions.

It uses class-type non-type template parameters, which let
\texttt{FixedString} serve as a template argument. It uses
\texttt{constexpr} lambdas and the brace of standard-library headers
that accompany the modern language.

Any recent release of GCC, Clang, or MSVC that advertises C++20 support
will do. If you are unsure of your toolchain, compile any small program
with \texttt{-std=c++20} first. Confirm the toolchain accepts it before
reaching for MuDSL.

Default header builds use standard C++ facilities and the embedded
\texttt{magic\_\allowbreak{}enum} headers. The byte-stream and language
facilities also use spans, fixed-width integers and standard containers.
Enabling \texttt{MUDSL\_\allowbreak{}ENABLE\_\allowbreak{}LUA} requires
Lua headers and linkage supplied by the user.

For \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}d}, use a D compiler with Phobos and
support for template mixins, \texttt{static foreach}, and CTFE. The
module has been exercised with LDC, DMD and GDC. Its optional native
bridge is selected with the \texttt{MuDSLLua} version identifier and Lua
linkage.

\section{A First D Program}\label{ch2-a-first-d-program}

Save this as \texttt{hello.\allowbreak{}d} alongside
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}d}:

\begin{code}
import MuDSL;
import std.stdio : writeln;

struct BasicDSL { mixin DSL!(Pipeline, Operators); }

void main()
{
    auto result = BasicDSL.wrap(5)
        | pipe((int x) => x + 3)
        | pipe((int x) => x * 2);
    writeln(result); // 16
}
\end{code}

\begin{code}
ldc2 hello.d MuDSL.d -of=hello
./hello
# Equivalent compiler invocations:
dmd hello.d MuDSL.d -of=hello
gdc hello.d MuDSL.d -o hello
\end{code}

Supply the module directory through \texttt{-I} when it is elsewhere.
The CMake installation includes both implementation files; its
\texttt{INTERFACE} target exports the C++ usage requirements. Chapter 31
provides the D feature guide.

\section{Obtaining the Header}\label{ch2-obtaining-the-header}

Obtaining the library means obtaining \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}.
In a typical source layout the header sits at the repository root. A
project that vendors MuDSL simply copies that one file into its own
include tree.

There is no separate source file to compile, no static library to build,
and no shared object to install for consumers. If your project already
has an \texttt{include/\allowbreak{}} or
\texttt{third\_\allowbreak{}party/\allowbreak{}} directory, dropping
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} there is sufficient.

Once the header is on your include path, using it is a single line:

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
\end{code}

That line pulls the entire \texttt{dsl} namespace into your translation
unit. Because the library is header-only, the cost is paid once per
translation unit at compile time. There is no link step and no runtime
library.

The \texttt{\#pragma once} guard at the top of the header prevents
double inclusion within a single translation unit. The include guards of
the standard library headers it pulls in prevent repeated work across
the rest of your build.

\section{A Direct Compiler Build}\label{ch2-a-direct-compiler-build}

The simplest possible build is a direct compiler invocation. Save the
following as \texttt{hello.\allowbreak{}cpp} next to a copy of
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp}:

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
    auto v = BasicDSL::wrap(5)
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
    std::cout << v << "\n";   // prints 16
}
\end{code}

Compile and run it in one step with GCC:

\begin{code}
g++ -std=c++20 hello.cpp -o hello
./hello
\end{code}

With Clang the command is identical in shape:

\begin{code}
clang++ -std=c++20 hello.cpp -o hello
./hello
\end{code}

That is the whole build for a consumer. No \texttt{-l} flags, no
\texttt{-I} flags beyond what your own layout needs, and no
\texttt{make}. The only mandatory flag is \texttt{-std=c++20} or a newer
standard.

Forgetting that flag is the single most common first-day problem. The
compiler will reject concepts and class-type NTTPs with errors that can
look bewildering if you do not know the cause.

If your project keeps headers in a separate directory, point the
compiler at it with \texttt{-I}. For example, if
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} lives in \texttt{include/\allowbreak{}}:

\begin{code}
g++ -std=c++20 -Iinclude src/hello.cpp -o hello
\end{code}

Many MuDSL snippets construct class-type non-type template parameters.
On some toolchains you may benefit from a larger template backtrace
limit to keep deep diagnostics navigable.

\begin{code}
g++ -std=c++20 -ftemplate-backtrace-limit=0 hello.cpp -o hello
\end{code}

These are quality-of-life flags, not requirements. They make error
messages easier to read; they do not change whether the code compiles.

\section{Consuming MuDSL Through
CMake}\label{ch2-consuming-mudsl-through-cmake}

For projects that already use CMake, MuDSL ships a ready-made
\texttt{INTERFACE} library target. The distribution's
\texttt{CMakeLists.\allowbreak{}txt} declares the target with a single
line and exports the include directory and the C++20 requirement as
usage requirements.

Consumers inherit these requirements automatically:

\begin{code}
project(MuDSL VERSION 1.0 LANGUAGES CXX)

add_library(MuDSL INTERFACE)

target_include_directories(MuDSL
    INTERFACE
        $<BUILD_INTERFACE:${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}>
        $<BUILD_INTERFACE:${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/..>
        $<INSTALL_INTERFACE:include>
)

target_compile_features(MuDSL INTERFACE cxx_std_20)
\end{code}

Because the target is \texttt{INTERFACE}, linking against it adds no
compiled object code. It only propagates the include path and the
\texttt{cxx\_\allowbreak{}std\_\allowbreak{}20} requirement.

A consuming project written against MuDSL therefore needs nothing more
than a few lines of its own:

\begin{code}
# Assuming MuDSL was added via add_subdirectory, FetchContent, or found
# through the installed package config.
add_executable(hello src/hello.cpp)
target_link_libraries(hello PRIVATE MuDSL)
\end{code}

Linking \texttt{MuDSL} is enough to bring in both the include directory
and the \texttt{-std=c++20} flag. CMake will not let you accidentally
build the target with an older standard, because
\texttt{target\_\allowbreak{}compile\_\allowbreak{}features(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{} INTERFACE cxx\_\allowbreak{}std\_\allowbreak{}20)\allowbreak{}}
makes C++20 a hard usage requirement.

\section{Installing and Finding the
Package}\label{ch2-installing-and-finding-the-package}

The distribution also installs a package configuration. Downstream
projects can then find MuDSL after it has been installed. The
\texttt{CMakeLists.\allowbreak{}txt} exports the target under the
\texttt{MuDSL:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}} namespace and generates a
\texttt{MuDSLConfig.\allowbreak{}cmake} from a template:

\begin{code}
include(CMakePackageConfigHelpers)
configure_package_config_file(
    cmake/MuDSLConfig.cmake.in
    "${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/MuDSLConfig.cmake"
    INSTALL_DESTINATION lib/cmake/MuDSL
)
\end{code}

After \texttt{cmake --install}, a downstream project can write:

\begin{code}
find_package(MuDSL REQUIRED)
add_executable(app src/main.cpp)
target_link_libraries(app PRIVATE MuDSL::MuDSL)
\end{code}

The generated config runs
\texttt{check\_\allowbreak{}required\_\allowbreak{}components} and
exposes \texttt{MuDSL\_\allowbreak{}INCLUDE\_\allowbreak{}DIR} and
\texttt{MuDSL\_\allowbreak{}INCLUDE\_\allowbreak{}DIRS} variables.
Projects that prefer to set include paths manually can read those
variables directly.

The config template is short. It includes the generated targets file and
sets the include-directory convenience variables from the package
prefix. Nothing in it is project-specific, so it works for any consumer
that has installed the package.

\section{Building the Bundled
Examples}\label{ch2-building-the-bundled-examples}

The source distribution includes an \texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}}
directory with two dozen numbered programs. Each one illustrates a
single feature or integration. They are the fastest way to see the
library in motion.

The examples are plain \texttt{.\allowbreak{}cpp} files that
\texttt{\#include "MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp"} and define a \texttt{main}.
You can compile any one of them directly:

\begin{code}
g++ -std=c++20 -I. examples/01-basic-usage.cpp -o ex01 && ./ex01
g++ -std=c++20 -I. examples/12-expression-templates.cpp -o ex12 && ./ex12
g++ -std=c++20 -I. examples/23-peg-definition.cpp -o ex23 && ./ex23
\end{code}

A convenient way to build them all at once is a tiny shell loop. This is
also a handy smoke test for your toolchain:

\begin{code}
mkdir -p build
for f in examples/*.cpp; do
  g++ -std=c++20 -I. "$f" -o "build/$(basename "$f" .cpp)" || echo "failed: $f"
done
\end{code}

The examples are numbered to roughly track the manual.
\texttt{01-basic-usage} matches the foundations of this chapter.
\texttt{02-fixed-strings\allowbreak{}} and \texttt{08-ast-creation}
match the AST chapters in Part IV.

The \texttt{22-} through \texttt{24-} examples correspond to the PEG
chapters in Part XII. Browsing the examples in parallel with the
relevant chapter is an effective way to learn, because each example is
small enough to read in full.

\section{Shared Language-Facility Examples and
Tests}\label{ch2-shared-language-facility-examples-and-tests}

The matching C++/D equality-saturation examples combine preprocessing,
S-expression rule loading, e-graphs, equality-triggered visitors,
explicit CFGs and static regexes. From the MuDSL directory:

\begin{code}
g++ -std=c++20 -I. examples/26-equality-saturation.cpp -o eqsat-cpp
./eqsat-cpp
ldc2 -I. MuDSL.d examples/26-equality-saturation.d -of=eqsat-d
./eqsat-d
\end{code}

The CMake example target is
\texttt{mudsl\_\allowbreak{}equality\_\allowbreak{}saturation\_\allowbreak{}example},
enabled by \texttt{BUILD\_\allowbreak{}EXAMPLES}.
\texttt{mudsl\_\allowbreak{}enum\_\allowbreak{}reflection\_\allowbreak{}example}
illustrates C++ reflection.

The standalone C++ test project provides enum and language-facility
coverage:

\begin{code}
cmake -S tests -B build/mudsl-tests
cmake --build build/mudsl-tests
ctest --test-dir build/mudsl-tests --output-on-failure
\end{code}

Set \texttt{-DMuDSL\_\allowbreak{}TEST\_\allowbreak{}LUA=ON} at
configuration to include native Lua preprocessing tests. Lua discovery
supplies the test target's include and link flags. D regression tests
live in the source module:

\begin{code}
ldc2 -unittest -main MuDSL.d -of=mudsl-test
./mudsl-test
\end{code}

Chapter 31 includes DMD/GDC verification commands, and Chapter 35
includes native Lua builds in both languages. Chapter 30 introduces all
shared APIs.

\section{Walking Through the First
Example}\label{ch2-walking-through-the-first-example}

Let us walk through the very first example in detail, since it
establishes conventions used throughout the manual.
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}01-basic-usage.\allowbreak{}cpp} is
reproduced in full below:

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

struct BasicDSL : dsl::DSL<BasicDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {};

int main() {
  auto v = BasicDSL::wrap(5) | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x + 3; })
           | dsl::pipe([](int x) { return x * 2; });
  auto gt5 = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x > 5; });
  auto even = BasicDSL::make_pred([](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });
  std::cout << v << " " << (gt5(v) && even(v)) << "\n";
}
\end{code}

Line by line: the \texttt{\#include} pulls in the toolkit. The
\texttt{struct} declaration defines an empty DSL that mixes in two
features.

\texttt{wrap(\allowbreak{}5)\allowbreak{}} lifts the integer \texttt{5}
into the pipeline. The two
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pipe(\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
stages add three then double. \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred}
constructs two predicates.

The final line prints the piped value and the conjunction of the two
predicate checks. The pipeline yields
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}5 + 3)\allowbreak{} * 2 = 16}, and
\texttt{16 \textgreater{}\allowbreak{} 5 \&\& 16 \% 2 == \allowbreak{}0}
is true, so the program prints \texttt{16 1}.

Notice that \texttt{BasicDSL} has an empty body yet still has behaviour.
The pipeline feature injects the static \texttt{wrap} method. The
operators feature injects the static \texttt{make\_\allowbreak{}pred}
factory.

The free-function \texttt{operator|} in the \texttt{dsl} namespace
handles the pipe. This separation --- features contribute methods, free
functions contribute operators --- recurs throughout the library.
Chapters 3 and 5 explore it fully.

\section{A Second Example: Expression
Templates}\label{ch2-a-second-example-expression-templates}

A second example worth building immediately is the expression-template
demo,
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}12-expression-te\allowbreak{}mplates.\allowbreak{}cpp}.
It defines a \texttt{Scalar} type that inherits
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ExprTemplates}.

The type stores a \texttt{double} and exposes
\texttt{expr\_\allowbreak{}value(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. The
arithmetic operators are then provided by the mixin:

\begin{code}
struct Scalar : dsl::DSL<Scalar, dsl::ExprTemplates> {
  double v{};
  Scalar() = default;
  explicit Scalar(double x) : v(x) {}
  double expr_value() const { return v; }
};

int main() {
  Scalar a{2}, b{3}, c{4};
  auto expr = a + b * c - a;
  std::cout << expr.eval() << "\n";   // 2 + 3*4 - 2 = 12
}
\end{code}

Compiling and running this confirms that the expression-template feature
built a lazy tree from \texttt{a + b * c - a}. The call to
\texttt{.\allowbreak{}eval(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} then reduced it
to \texttt{12}.

Chapter 15 and Chapter 16 explain the mechanism. For now, treat the
example as proof that the toolkit compiles and runs on your machine.

\section{A Third Example: The PEG
Engine}\label{ch2-a-third-example-the-peg-engine}

A third example,
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}23-peg-definitio\allowbreak{}n.\allowbreak{}cpp},
exercises the heaviest subsystem --- the Parsing Expression Grammar
engine. It creates a grammar, adds rules with compile-time pattern
strings, and parses a sentence.

Whitespace is routed to an ignore channel so it is matched but not
reported as a token:

\begin{code}
auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition();
auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t\\n]+">([](dsl::PEGMatch &m){
    std::cout << "skip_ws:" << m.length() << "\n";
});
ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;
auto &kw_if = grammar.add_rule<"if">([](dsl::PEGMatch &m){
    std::cout << "kw:" << m.value << "\n";
});
auto result = grammar.parse("if x1 + 2 then 40");
std::cout << (result.ok() ? "parse_ok" : "parse_failed") << "\n";
\end{code}

Building this example is a good smoke test that your toolchain's C++20
support is complete enough for the whole library. The PEG engine
exercises concepts, variadic templates, and \texttt{constexpr} pattern
compilation.

Parts X through XII cover the parser and grammar machinery in depth. If
this example compiles and runs, everything in between will too.

\section{Common Build Problems}\label{ch2-common-build-problems}

When a build fails, the cause is almost always one of a small number of
things. The first is the standard version. If you see errors about
\texttt{concepts}, \texttt{requires}, or
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}same\_\allowbreak{}as}, you have
forgotten \texttt{-std=c++20}.

The second is the include path. If the compiler reports
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp:\allowbreak{} No such file or\allowbreak{} directory},
the header is not where the compiler is looking. Add the right
\texttt{-I} flag or fix the
\texttt{target\_\allowbreak{}include\_\allowbreak{}directories} entry.

The third is an old compiler. A toolchain that predates solid class-type
NTTP support will choke on
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf\textless{}\allowbreak{}"tag"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}".\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
even with \texttt{-std=c++20}. The fix is to upgrade to a current
release of your compiler.

A subtler class of error involves template diagnostics. Because MuDSL is
template-heavy, a mistake in your own code can produce a wall of
instantiation traceback. Two habits help you cope.

First, build the smallest example that reproduces the problem in
isolation, away from your real codebase. Second, read the \emph{first}
error in the output, not the last.

The first error is usually the root cause. Everything after it is
cascading fallout. The bundled examples give you a known-good baseline
to diff against when something goes wrong.

\section{A Productive Learning
Workflow}\label{ch2-a-productive-learning-workflow}

Another useful habit is to keep a throwaway translation unit that you
compile often while learning. MuDSL's features are independent, so you
can grow this file one feature at a time.

Add the pipeline today, the operators tomorrow, and pattern matching the
day after. Confirm each compiles before stacking the next. This
incremental approach matches the manual's part structure and keeps
feedback loops short.

If you integrate MuDSL into a larger project, consider wrapping it
behind your own thin header. Do not include
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} directly from many places.

The library's own overview comment suggests exactly this. Define your
DSL type in \texttt{mydsl.\allowbreak{}hpp}, include
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} there, and have the rest of your code
include only \texttt{mydsl.\allowbreak{}hpp}.

\begin{code}
// mydsl.hpp — the only file that sees MuDSL.hpp directly
#include "MuDSL.hpp"

struct MathDSL : dsl::DSL<MathDSL, dsl::Pipeline, dsl::Operators> {
    // domain-specific surface here
};
\end{code}

This localises the toolkit. It lets you adjust your DSL's surface
without touching callers, and it keeps your build's include graph clean.

\section{Build Performance}\label{ch2-build-performance}

Build performance is rarely a concern for MuDSL itself. The header is
modest in size, and the templates it instantiates are shallow.

The expression templates and parser combinators are the most
instantiation-heavy parts. If compile time matters in a large project,
factor heavy DSL usage into a few translation units rather than
spreading it across many.

For typical use, the difference is imperceptible. A program that
includes the header and uses a couple of features compiles in well under
a second on modern hardware.

\section{Summary}\label{ch2-summary}

Put \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} on your include path and compile
with \texttt{-std=c++20}; default builds need no extra library linkage.
With CMake, link the \texttt{MuDSL} \texttt{INTERFACE} target and
inherit its usage requirements. For D, place
\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}d} on the import path and compile it
alongside the application. Optional native Lua builds add the Lua
dependency described in Chapter 35.

Build a bundled example first to confirm the toolchain, then write your
own \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL} subclass. The next
chapter opens the black box of that base class.

You will see how CRTP and the feature mixins turn one line of
inheritance into a fully featured language. By now, though, you should
have a compiled MuDSL program running on your machine and a feel for the
three-step loop introduced in Chapter 1: inherit
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}DSL}, add your domain members,
and express logic through the operators those features provide.

Everything that follows in the manual adds depth to that loop, one
subsystem at a time. If your first program ran, you are ready to begin.
